---
/**
 * THE TRANSLATION NOTICE. Antonio's idea, 2026-08-04.
 *
 * His brief: when someone lands on the Portuguese or Spanish site, tell them
 * once that the original is English and that this version is AI-assisted, ask
 * them to be gentle about anything that reads wrong, and invite them to
 * report it. He suggested a light joke about the robot team, which is right:
 * an apology reads as a warning about quality, a joke reads as confidence
 * plus honesty, and honesty about how this was made is the same instinct that
 * made the site say "no official answer exists" instead of inventing one.
 *
 * IT TOOK THREE DRAFTS AND THE THIRD IS THE RIGHT ONE.
 *
 * Draft 1 said "our team of robots". Antonio: "otherwise it seems like
 * everything is made by AI, and it is not." Correct, and the cost would have
 * dwarfed the joke. This site's whole trust argument is human experience,
 * almost four decades of it, written by DJs who have stood next to the
 * failure. A visitor's first sentence in their own language saying "robots"
 * hands that away, and no amount of good copy downstream buys it back.
 *
 * Draft 2 led with the DJs and demoted the machines to a joke. Better, still
 * wrong, and Antonio saw why: "you can even take out AI or robots if you
 * want. The important thing is to let them know that this is made with love.
 * It's not perfect, we'll never be, but we want it to be every time close to
 * perfection, and their help is very welcome."
 *
 * That is the whole message, and none of it depends on the method. Naming the
 * tooling up front does active harm: it makes a reader scan for mistakes
 * instead of reading, and it invites them to discount the content before the
 * first paragraph. We do not tell anyone which text editor the English was
 * written in either. Nothing here is a lie by omission: the notice says
 * plainly that the original is English and that this version is new, and if
 * anyone asks how it was made we answer straight.
 *
 * What is left is the truth that matters to the reader: this was made with
 * care, it is not perfect yet, and their correction is genuinely wanted. That
 * is also the brand's oldest instinct, the same one behind "no official
 * answer exists" and "made by DJs for DJs": say the honest thing, then ask
 * the community for help.
 *
 * FOUR RULES IT OBEYS, all of them load-bearing:
 *
 * 1. NEVER IN AN EMERGENCY. It does not render on /emergency, anywhere under
 *    /protocol, or on the outcome pages. The Emergency Engine's founding rule
 *    is that nothing competes with the decision on screen, and a DJ two
 *    minutes from a set does not need a note about translation quality. This
 *    is enforced in the component, not left to whoever adds a page.
 *
 * 2. ONCE, EVER, PER LANGUAGE. Dismissal is remembered in localStorage. A
 *    notice that returns is an interruption, not a courtesy.
 *
 * 3. IT IS A STRIP, NOT A MODAL. Nothing is blocked, nothing is centred over
 *    the page, no overlay. It sits in the flow at the top of main and can be
 *    ignored completely.
 *
 * 4. IT SHIPS HIDDEN AND IS REVEALED BY SCRIPT. Rendering it visible and
 *    hiding it on load would move the page after paint, which is the layout
 *    shift we just spent a pass removing. Hidden first, shown only if the
 *    visitor has not dismissed it, so a returning reader never sees a flash.
 *
 * The report link goes to the language's own /feedback page. It was a mailto
 * while /pt/feedback and /es/feedback did not exist; they do now, and a form
 * in the reader's language beats an email composer.
 */
import { DEFAULT_LANG, noticeSilent } from '../i18n/registry';

interface Props {
  lang: string;
  /** English canonical path of the page, used only to stay out of the flow. */
  path: string;
}
const { lang, path } = Astro.props;

// Rule 1, in code. The list itself moved to src/i18n/registry.js as
// NOTICE_SILENT on 2026-08-05: LangUndo needs the same answer, and so does the
// wrapper in Base.astro, and three copies of one list is three chances to
// drift. Extend it THERE rather than adding conditions at call sites.
const show = lang !== DEFAULT_LANG && !noticeSilent(path);

// RULE 6 needs to know it is on the homepage. See the note beside it in the
// script below: only the homepage has three doors competing for the first
// screen, so only the homepage yields.
const isHome = path === '/';

// Antonio, 2026-08-05: reworded from an apology ("ainda não está perfeita")
// to continuous improvement ("em constante evolução"). The CTA is the closing
// sentence of the lead, rendered in brand red, and now that /pt/feedback and
// /es/feedback exist it links there instead of the old mailto.
const COPY = {
  pt: {
    lead: 'O Save My Gig nasceu em inglês e a versão em português está em constante evolução. Encontrou alguma tradução estranha?',
    cta: 'Conte pra gente.',
    href: '/pt/feedback',
    close: 'Fechar aviso',
  },
  es: {
    lead: 'Save My Gig nació en inglés y la versión en español está en constante evolución. ¿Encontraste alguna traducción extraña?',
    cta: 'Cuéntanos.',
    href: '/es/feedback',
    close: 'Cerrar aviso',
  },
};
const c = COPY[lang];
---
{show && c && (
  <div class="tx-notice" id="txNotice" data-lang={lang} hidden>
    <div class="tx-body">
      <p class="tx-lead">{c.lead} <a
        class="tx-cta"
        href={c.href}
        data-event="translation_report"
        data-lang={lang}
      >{c.cta}</a></p>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="tx-close" id="txClose" aria-label={c.close}>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 6 6 18M6 6l12 12"/></svg>
    </button>
  </div>
)}

{show && c && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lang, isHome }}>
    (function () {
      var el = document.getElementById('txNotice');
      if (!el) return;

      // RULE 5, ADDED 2026-08-05: THE LANGUAGE UNDO OUTRANKS THIS.
      // window.__smgAutoLang is set by the detection script in Base.astro, and
      // only on the pageview an automatic root redirect just landed on. On that
      // one pageview LangUndo.astro shows a line telling the visitor they were
      // moved and offering the way back, and two grey strips stacked above the
      // h1 is one too many: measured at 390x844, the pair puts the third
      // homepage door's bottom edge at 764px, past the ~736px a real first load
      // has once the URL bar is expanded. A door a DJ cannot see is a door they
      // do not know exists, and that outranks a note about translation quality.
      // Nothing is lost by yielding: this notice is only ever marked seen when
      // it is DISMISSED, so it appears in full on the next page they open.
      // Checked here rather than by LangUndo reaching in and hiding #txNotice,
      // because this script runs last and would simply undo that.
      if (window.__smgAutoLang) return;

      // RULE 6, ADDED 2026-08-06: ON THE HOMEPAGE, THE THIRD DOOR OUTRANKS THIS.
      // Rule 5 above is the same judgement against a different competitor, and
      // the sentence it turns on is the one that matters here too: a door a DJ
      // cannot see is a door they do not know exists.
      //
      // This strip costs 125px, and it spends them at the very top of the page,
      // above the h1. On a 375x667 iPhone SE the first screen is about 559px
      // once the URL bar is expanded, so those 125px are 22% of everything the
      // reader gets on load. Measured on /pt and /es at that size, the third
      // door's bottom edge lands at 557px with four of the five rotating
      // taglines and 560px with the fifth: two pixels of room, then one pixel
      // short. English, which never renders this strip, has 141px of room on
      // the same phone. The strip IS the difference, and the doors were only
      // ever passing by luck.
      //
      // Buying it back inside the strip is not possible: it has already been
      // through that pass, 227px down to 125px, and what is left is one
      // paragraph of the smallest type on the site. The remaining choice is
      // which of the two things loses, and the project answered that in Rule 5.
      //
      // SCOPED TIGHT, deliberately. Only the homepage, because only the
      // homepage puts three doors on the first screen, and only under 620px of
      // viewport height, which in the gate's device list is the iPhone SE alone
      // (the next shortest phone loads with 672px and has 61px of room). Every
      // other page and every other phone is untouched.
      //
      // Nothing is lost by yielding, for the same reason Rule 5 loses nothing:
      // this notice is marked seen only when it is DISMISSED, never when it is
      // merely skipped, so the reader gets it in full on the next page they
      // open, where no door is waiting on those 125px. Read at load, while the
      // URL bar is still expanded, which is the moment the fold is tightest.
      if (isHome && window.innerHeight <= 620) return;

      var KEY = 'SMG_TX_NOTICE_' + lang;
      var seen = false;
      try { seen = localStorage.getItem(KEY) === '1'; } catch (e) { /* private mode */ }
      // Shown only when unseen. A returning reader never gets a flash because
      // the element ships hidden and is only ever revealed, never hidden back.
      if (!seen) el.hidden = false;
      var close = document.getElementById('txClose');
      if (close) close.addEventListener('click', function () {
        el.hidden = true;
        try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, '1'); } catch (e) { /* nothing to do */ }
      });
    })();
  </script>
)}

<style is:global>
  /* The layout renders this above the page's own .wrap, so it carries the
     site container itself. Padding-bottom only: the page below already
     brings --page-top. */
  .tx-wrap {
    max-width: var(--site-w); margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--page-top) var(--site-pad) 0;
  }
  /* THE DEAD BOX, PART TWO (2026-08-06). Part one removed the wrapper at BUILD
     time wherever no strip could ever render, which fixed every English page and
     every pt/es tunnel page. It did not fix the common case, and the comments
     claiming it was "removed sitewide" were wrong.
     Both strips ship hidden and are revealed by script, and the translation
     notice is revealed only until the reader DISMISSES it. From that moment on,
     for that reader, every pt and es page renders a wrapper whose children are
     all display:none and whose padding-top is still there. Measured at 390x844
     on /pt/checklist: 125px tall with the notice up and the h1 at 260px, then
     24px tall with the h1 at 142px after one dismissal and a reload. 24px of
     nothing under the nav on every translated page, for every returning reader,
     which is the majority state after a first visit.
     A build-time check cannot know about a runtime dismissal, so this is the
     runtime half: the padding exists only while a strip is actually drawable.
     WRITTEN AS :not(:has(...)) RATHER THAN THE OTHER WAY ROUND, deliberately. A
     browser without :has() ignores the rule and keeps exactly today's spacing,
     so its worst case is the old cosmetic box rather than a strip jammed against
     the nav. The site already depends on :has() in global.css for the sticky CTA
     stack, so this is not a new dependency, just a careful use of one. */
  .tx-wrap:not(:has(> .tx-notice:not([hidden]))) { padding-top: 0; }
  /* Kept as a backstop and nothing more. It has never once matched: Astro leaves
     a newline where a top-level {cond && ...} renders nothing, and :empty counts
     whitespace text nodes. That is the bug the rule above exists to finish. */
  .tx-wrap:empty { display: none; }

  /* Quiet by construction: the amber rail marks it as information, the same
     way the site's advisory panels do, and nothing here competes with the
     page's own first heading. */
  /* NO COLOURED RAIL. The first version used the amber rail the site's
     advisory panels use, which was wrong on the site's own terms: amber means
     "this is costing you". This is a welcome, not a warning, so it takes the
     plain panel treatment and lets the link carry the only colour. */
  .tx-notice {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.75rem;
    border: var(--stroke) solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surf);
    padding: 0.7rem 0.75rem 0.7rem 0.85rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .tx-notice[hidden] { display: none; }
  .tx-body { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; }
  /* HEIGHT IS A FEATURE HERE. The first version was three blocks and cost
     227px at 390px wide, which pushed the homepage's third door BELOW THE
     FOLD on a 360x740 Android, the exact device class the homepage CSS calls
     out as common in Brazil. A note about translation quality is not worth a
     door. One paragraph, inline link, tighter box: about 110px. */
  .tx-lead {
    margin: 0; font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .tx-joke {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .tx-cta {
    font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--red); text-decoration: none;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
  .tx-cta:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .tx-close {
    flex: 0 0 auto; width: 44px; height: 44px; margin: -0.5rem -0.4rem 0 0;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background: none; border: 0; color: var(--text-faint); cursor: pointer;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .tx-close:hover { color: var(--text); }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .tx-lead, .tx-joke { font-size: 0.82rem; }
  }
  @media print { .tx-notice { display: none !important; } }
</style>
